        "fr-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "d4da77e7-30c8-347d-8204-7dce9a473593",
                "word": "télique",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "0291c9d7-015a-437e-b528-98cb2ab6089d",
                        "entry_id": "d4da77e7-30c8-347d-8204-7dce9a473593",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "masculine",
                            "feminine"
                        ],
                        "form": "téliques"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e0d6e803-9478-3755-b5a4-4968d6fdb314",
                        "entry_id": "d4da77e7-30c8-347d-8204-7dce9a473593",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qui présente un évènement ou un procès mené à son terme."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8bbe9089-3b06-40ce-9cc3-57470d2680d5",
                                "sense_id": "e0d6e803-9478-3755-b5a4-4968d6fdb314",
                                "text": "Un événement – ainsi que le groupe verbal qui l’exprime – est télique si son développement est orienté vers une fin naturelle (c’est-à-dire, une fin intrinsèque ou envisagée) au-delà de laquelle l’événement en question ne peut plus continuer."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6b953bf1-6093-4c75-b18c-4989da5d8b69",
                                "sense_id": "e0d6e803-9478-3755-b5a4-4968d6fdb314",
                                "text": "La recherche portant sur les facteurs qui conditionnent l’apparition d’une interprétation télique ou non télique pour un énoncé donné s’est orientée d’abord vers les verbes."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0aac2d0e-b602-383e-b03c-23a72349c5ce",
                        "entry_id": "d4da77e7-30c8-347d-8204-7dce9a473593",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Orienté vers un but."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "be82c80b-ffd9-48b3-9238-fb84b2688543",
                                "sense_id": "0aac2d0e-b602-383e-b03c-23a72349c5ce",
                                "text": "Le cheminement allant de l’anxiété à la relaxation est appelé mode télique, tandis que le cheminement allant de l’ennui à l’excitation correspond au mode paratélique."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a5975b94-23d3-4bfb-934b-ce995cca870c",
                                "sense_id": "0aac2d0e-b602-383e-b03c-23a72349c5ce",
                                "text": "La valeur principale de l’état télique est l’accomplissement de ces objectifs dans le futur, au besoin au détriment du plaisir immédiat."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "8eb70558-5ad7-49bd-ac9c-0ed1c35d020b",
                        "source_entry_id": "d4da77e7-30c8-347d-8204-7dce9a473593",
                        "target_lang_word": "telic",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "telic",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3ac9762f-b0e0-348e-9265-b95ef90b19fc",
                                        "entry_id": "52f165a5-50c0-31e2-ac32-605a36786984",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Tending or directed towards a goal or specific end."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e03b073a-869f-4b62-b934-cbff4090593c",
                                                "sense_id": "3ac9762f-b0e0-348e-9265-b95ef90b19fc",
                                                "text": "Several theorists of the previous chapters are supportive of this more telic view of human nature."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7e41c9d9-61d8-45e7-ade9-1fbd5bf36bd9",
                                                "sense_id": "3ac9762f-b0e0-348e-9265-b95ef90b19fc",
                                                "text": "They were asked to rate the 36 activities for how purposeful they were.[…]Comparing the 10 most telic and the 10 most paratelic we found that the paratelic leisure activities were thought to involve less skill or challenge; they were also judged to satisfy social needs more, and to be more enjoyable."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c944d2cc-2e73-4805-aafb-df687e30f66d",
                                                "sense_id": "3ac9762f-b0e0-348e-9265-b95ef90b19fc",
                                                "text": "I have certainly become more telic as I strive to achieve my goals set, but I am not really enjoying any of it."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2d997960-a4d2-3e49-85ae-222d2f8472af",
                                        "entry_id": "52f165a5-50c0-31e2-ac32-605a36786984",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "That expresses an end or purpose."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "541cbbec-10d9-4f6e-ae44-bc38118958f1",
                                                "sense_id": "2d997960-a4d2-3e49-85ae-222d2f8472af",
                                                "text": "1995, Michela Cennamo, Patterns of 'Active' Syntax in Late Latin Pleonastic Reflexives, John Charles Smith, Delia Bentley (editors), Historical Linguistics 1995: Selected Papers from the 12th International Conference on Historical Linguistics, Volume 1: General Issues and Non-Germanic Languages, page 39,\nIn this framework, verbs denoting directed change of location, such as Italian andare 'go', instantiate Core Unaccusativity, in that they have a Theme subject and are the most telic, concrete, dynamic."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a424b961-c78c-40ce-913a-92dace464fc4",
                                                "sense_id": "2d997960-a4d2-3e49-85ae-222d2f8472af",
                                                "text": "Similarly, verb forms that can govern either transitive or middle-case marking (cf. 2.1.3.1.2(c)) are more telic in their transitive manifestations."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b36f0838-6855-4d96-b146-f3a0b9aa9bd3",
                                                "sense_id": "2d997960-a4d2-3e49-85ae-222d2f8472af",
                                                "text": "Moreover, let us remember that some verbs can be telic and agentive at the same time: if one looks at the ASH category n°1 (change of location), i.e. the verbs considered the most telic, like FF arriver (arrive), partir (leave), venir (come), revenir (come back) (Sorace 2000:256), old Spanish huir (run away) and escapar (escape) (Legendre 2007), do they not bear an agentive component?"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "24e4666c-dc47-3c2e-a518-3eded1bf67ce",
                                        "entry_id": "52f165a5-50c0-31e2-ac32-605a36786984",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "That expresses the perfective aspect."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
